---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import SqlInjectionChecklist from '../../components/SqlInjectionChecklist.jsx';
---
<ToolLayout title="SQL Injection Test Checklist" description="An interactive checklist for reviewing SQL injection prevention and testing before shipping.">
  <SqlInjectionChecklist client:load slot="tool" />

  <h2>Why SQL injection persists despite being well-understood</h2>
  <p>SQL injection has been a known vulnerability class for decades, yet it remains common — usually not because parameterized queries are unknown, but because one overlooked raw string concatenation somewhere in a large codebase is enough to reintroduce the vulnerability.</p>

  <h2>How to use this checklist</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Review prevention practices across your codebase, not just new code.</li>
    <li>Actively test with common payloads rather than just reviewing code by eye.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Common mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Assuming an ORM automatically prevents all injection</strong> — raw query methods within an ORM can still be vulnerable if used with string concatenation.</li>
    <li><strong>Testing only string input fields</strong>, missing that numeric fields can also be vulnerable to injection with the right payload.</li>
    <li><strong>Relying solely on input validation</strong> without parameterized queries — validation helps but isn't a substitute for the core defense.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Use cases</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Reviewing a codebase for SQL injection vulnerabilities before a security audit</li>
    <li>Establishing secure coding standards for a development team</li>
    <li>Testing an application's input handling as part of a penetration test</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>FAQ</h2>
  <p><strong>Does using an ORM guarantee I'm safe from SQL injection?</strong> Mostly, for standard query builder methods — but raw/native query escape hatches most ORMs provide can still be vulnerable if user input is concatenated directly into them.</p>
</ToolLayout>
